<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.jenkins-ci.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>plugin</artifactId>
    <version>6.2211.v27f680c93c53</version>
    <relativePath />
  </parent>

  <groupId>io.github.rads4</groupId>
  <artifactId>ck-aws</artifactId>
  <version>${revision}${changelist}</version>
  <packaging>hpi</packaging>

  <name>CK AWS Plugin</name>
  <description>Centralizes AWS authentication and generic AWS CLI execution for Jenkins deployment pipelines.</description>

  <properties>
    <!--
      SEMANTIC VERSIONING: major.minor.patch, adopted 2026-08-17.

        major - a breaking change to the configuration contract or to what the plugin
                guarantees about a build. Removing a form entry is NOT major while the
                property still loads from existing XML.
        minor - new capability, or a change in WHAT gets attributed.
        patch - defect fix, or a UI/documentation change with no behaviour change.

      This number is PROVISIONAL and is confirmed at install time, not now.

      VERSIONS TRACK INSTALLATIONS, NOT BUILDS.
      The number must change before an artifact is INSTALLED on a controller, so that
      "the controller says 2.2.0" answers "which 2.2.0" unambiguously. A number that has
      been installed is spent forever and must never be reused. A number only ever built
      locally is not spent, and may be re-taken by the build that actually ships.

      History, so the record is not confusing. Under the old two-component scheme:
        2.1  - installed on CK production (infra Jenkins). Master switch off.
        2.2  - installed on the POC clone (sha256 f5150ba3...). The build validated
               against real jobs: every canary, all 7 agent types, dev2/fluentd #119.
        2.3  - installed on the POC clone (sha256 bc4d59e1...). Static ARN form entry
               removed. Both POC installs are test installs; both numbers are spent.
        2.4  - built, never installed. Node-label form entry removed.
      Several earlier artifacts also called themselves 2.2/2.3/2.4 and are VOID - the
      first of them still contained the DynamicContext shadowing defect. See MEMORY.md.

      NUMBERING FROM HERE (decided 2026-08-17):
        POC iterations run on the 2.1.x line - 2.1.1, 2.1.2, ... - because infra Jenkins
        is on 2.1 and these are patches on top of what it already runs. Each POC install
        spends one patch number, which is cheap.
        The INFRA install claims 2.2.0. That number is deliberately NOT spent on the POC
        clone, so "the controller says 2.2.0" can only ever mean the infra release.
      Prefer a value that cannot be confused with a spent two-component version when read
      on a controller.

      Build a release, not a private snapshot: `mvn -Dchangelist= clean verify` produces a
      versioned ck-aws whose version string is meaningful on a controller. A default build
      is `-SNAPSHOT (private-<hash>-<user>)`, which is deliberately NOT installable - that
      is what keeps the number provisional until installation.
    -->
    <revision>2.1.8</revision>
    <changelist>-SNAPSHOT</changelist>
    <!--
      Baseline is pinned to CloudKeeper's actual Jenkins version (2.479.2) rather than the
      current recommended LTS, so the plugin is guaranteed loadable on CK's real instance.
      Note this is deliberately BELOW the archetype default (2.528.3) and below the update
      center distribution floor (~2.504.2) - acceptable because the POC is never published.
      https://www.jenkins.io/doc/developer/plugin-development/choosing-jenkins-baseline/
    -->
    <jenkins.baseline>2.479</jenkins.baseline>
    <jenkins.version>${jenkins.baseline}.2</jenkins.version>

    <spotless.check.skip>false</spotless.check.skip>
    <ban-junit4-imports.skip>false</ban-junit4-imports.skip>
    <ban-commons-lang-2.skip>false</ban-commons-lang-2.skip>
    <hpi.strictBundledArtifacts>true</hpi.strictBundledArtifacts>
  </properties>

  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>io.jenkins.tools.bom</groupId>
        <artifactId>bom-${jenkins.baseline}.x</artifactId>
        <!--
          Pinned deliberately, NOT to the newest BOM release. Every BOM after this one pins
          workflow-cps >= 4050, whose manifest requires Jenkins 2.479.3 - which would force the
          jenkins.version baseline above CK production (2.479.2) and break the M0 guarantee that the
          plugin is loadable on CK's real instance. 4488 is the newest tested-together set whose
          workflow-cps (4046.v90b_1b_9edec67) still requires only 2.479.1.
          Revisit once CK moves to 2.479.3+.
        -->
        <version>4488.v7fe26526366e</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>
    <!--
      Pipeline step API only: Step / StepDescriptor / StepExecution. Versions are managed by the
      Jenkins plugin BOM above, so none are pinned here.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.jenkins-ci.plugins.workflow</groupId>
      <artifactId>workflow-step-api</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      Test-only: enough to define and run a Pipeline job in JenkinsRule tests.

      workflow-durable-task-step was added in M6, because ckAwsWithProfile requires Launcher/FilePath
      context - i.e. it only runs inside a 'node' block, so the tests must be able to open one and run
      'sh' inside it. Its version comes from the BOM above, so the 2.479.2 baseline still holds.

      Deliberately NOT workflow-basic-steps (which would provide 'echo'): it drags in
      instance-identity 203.x, which declares Jenkins-Version 2.479.3 and would raise this plugin's
      baseline above CK production 2.479.2. Tests print with 'sh' instead - which is a stronger console
      masking test anyway, since it exercises real subprocess output rather than Jenkins' own logger.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.jenkins-ci.plugins.workflow</groupId>
      <artifactId>workflow-cps</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.jenkins-ci.plugins.workflow</groupId>
      <artifactId>workflow-durable-task-step</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.jenkins-ci.plugins.workflow</groupId>
      <artifactId>workflow-job</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      NOT added: io.jenkins:configuration-as-code (test scope), which would let a test load a real JCasC
      YAML document. Every available version drags in BOM-managed transitives (instance-identity 203.x,
      and JCasC itself from 1947 onwards) that declare Jenkins-Version 2.479.3 - which would raise this
      plugin's baseline above CK production 2.479.2 and break the M0 loadability guarantee.

      The plugin has no compile-time dependency on JCasC and does not need one: JCasC configures any
      GlobalConfiguration through @Symbol + @DataBoundSetter + structs' DescribableModel. That is
      exactly the surface CkAwsGlobalConfigurationTest asserts against, using structs directly (already
      on the classpath) - the same mechanism JCasC would use, minus the baseline conflict. Revisit once
      CK moves to 2.479.3+, together with the BOM pin above.
    -->
  </dependencies>

  <repositories>
    <repository>
      <id>repo.jenkins-ci.org</id>
      <url>https://repo.jenkins-ci.org/public/</url>
    </repository>
  </repositories>
  <pluginRepositories>
    <pluginRepository>
      <id>repo.jenkins-ci.org</id>
      <url>https://repo.jenkins-ci.org/public/</url>
    </pluginRepository>
  </pluginRepositories>
</project>
